/* Data packs for blocks/role-marking.html (prim:role-marking): role-marking-en, role-marking-ja, role-marking-zh.
 *
 * Pack format
 *   markerPos    "post" (ja: particle after the noun) | "pre" (en, zh: preposition before the noun). A noun may override
 *                it with its own "pos" (zh locatives 上 / 里 come after the noun).
 *   space        "" or " ": what goes between a marker and its noun when the sentence is assembled.
 *   stop         sentence-final punctuation used in the build step.
 *   markerOrder  the fixed order of marker buttons. "—" = no marker (a real answer in some items).
 *   markers      { marker: {means, geo?, note, noteEn} }. `means` = the role this marker usually signals (or "none");
 *                when the learner picks a wrong marker, the scene redraws the noun in that role (red dashed outline)
 *                and the note says what the marker means. `geo` (in / on / under / at / beside) redraws a location.
 *   roleMarkers  { role: [markers] } which markers fit which role; used only to check the learner's own sentence.
 *   buildMarkers marker buttons offered in the build step.
 *   items        [{id, kc, s, nouns, gloss, r?, why?, frame?, pair?, child?, decoy?, line?}]
 *                s      sentence template; {k} is replaced by noun k plus its slot.
 *                nouns  [{k, w, r?, pic, role, mark?, opts?, fixed?, geo?, pos?, note?}] role = the right role (or a list
 *                       when two roles are both defensible); mark = right marker(s); a noun without `mark` has no slot.
 *                       fixed = text shown after the noun that is not a gap (ja topic は). r = reading (kana / pinyin).
 *                gloss  {zh, en} meaning or scene description; shown only when the interface language differs from
 *                       the pack language, and always used as the picture's text description.
 *                why    {k, ans, ansEn?, note, noteEn}: after the markers, "what decides the marker at k?" — role |
 *                       verb (the verb or word itself fixes it) | split (one word in the learner's first language
 *                       splits here). ansEn when the answer differs for an English-speaking learner. This is the
 *                       reverse half: same role, different marker; marker fixed by the verb.
 *                frame  {t, note, noteEn} verb frame card (shown after a wrong choice or as hint 3).
 *                pair   id of an item with the same noun in a different role (kept next to each other in a round).
 *                child  usable in the child version (only simple spatial roles).
 *                decoy  extra roles shown in the role ring.  line: "time" draws a time line instead of a place.
 *   builds       [{id, verb, verbPic, gloss, order, nouns:[{k, w, r?, pic, roles}], frame?, frameCard?}] step 3:
 *                a new picture; nouns with roles [] are not in the picture. order = roles in sentence order, "VERB"
 *                where the verb goes. frame overrides roleMarkers for this verb.
 *   reverse      text for the side card on where roles stop predicting markers.
 *   review       items a native teacher should check.
 */
